data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1793186999328,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16, 1969 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Moon\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 fulfilled a goal set by President Kennedy in May 1961 to land a man on the Moon before the decade ended.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury program\",\"Gemini program\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21 while Armstrong and Aldrin were on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, among the most-watched television events in history.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples from the mission led to the identification of three previously unrecognized minerals.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The command module Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum, while Eagle's descent stage remains on the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth; Alan Shepard followed on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"United States\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"NASA\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ends.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important for long-range space exploration, and none more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding other engines and unmanned explorations.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says that putting a man on the Moon will be a national effort, since all Americans must work to get him there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argues that outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards threaten everyone alike.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says conquering space deserves the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answers the question of why go to the Moon by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Texas\",\"Atlantic\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the US chose to go to the Moon this decade because the challenge is hard, and it would organize the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener at MIT dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was already named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing project to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon expedition again at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the joint idea was abandoned after his death.\"},{\"id\":\"7.6\",\"text\":\"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"Lunar orbit rendezvous was announced by NASA head James Webb in July 1962 as the Apollo mission approach.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three parts: command module (only part returning to Earth), service module (propulsion, power, oxygen, water), and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"This design allowed the spacecraft to be launched by a single Saturn V rocket then under development.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Apollo was enabled by NASA's adoption of MOSFETs in the Interplanetary Monitoring Platform and silicon IC chips in the Apollo Guidance Computer.\"}],\"entities\":[\"NASA\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee and abruptly halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Moon\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury program\",\"Gemini program\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"Atlantic\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"6655d6c159f1737c7b3e3917c474a1193e11240f28c33f257379abaf6e5edc5c"}}

